                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "This game has some flaws, but if you are a fan of tetris and have a vive this title is certainly worth the low asking price.\n\n3 boards may seem intimidating at first, but you can freely move falling blocks to any grid. Also unlike classic tetris games, you can not only rotate a block, but flip it as well. This makes it easier to fit blocks quickly.\n\nAside from fun gameplay, it is also a great light cardio workout (which means you need a decently sized play area to actually play this game). You'll be reaching high, turning rapidly, and ducking low to construct as many lines as possible.\n\nAs for the flaws, the game severly lacks in customization. Being able to change the background image, or at least the color would really make this that much more pleasant to play. While gray does help keep you from getting distracted, the option to jazz it up would be very welcomed.\n\nMy biggest complaint in customization though is the inability to toggle the music on/off. The ingame music has very few tracks and they are highly repetative. This is the kind of game that most people are going to want to play while listening to their own music selection.\n\nMy last complaint could be a tech issue on my end so take it with a grain of salt, but I've noticed that once you get around level 20, the headset gets more and more unstable. Granted I don't have the most powerful PC on the market, but it's been more than capable of playing other VR games with higher detail and maintaing steady frames. What's strange is this game starts smooth, but the longer it's played  the more unstable it seems to become.\n\nStill, even with that issue the game is really fun to play, and for only $4.00 it's worth dealing with the problems.",
